      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="295" width="140" height="100" textColor="666666" fontName="Arial" fontSize="10" borderColor="9900CC" borderStyle="Dashed" borderWidth="2.0" fillColor="F5E6FF" shapeType="Rectangle" zOrder="-1" />
      <Comment>Both CYP79F1 and CYP79F2 are involved in methionine-derived aliphatic glucosinolates biosynthesis. CYP79F1 can metabolize homo, di, tri, tetra, penta, and hexahomomethionine to their corresponding aldoximes, and has higher substrate specificity for di or trihomomethionine. CYP79F2, on the other hand, can only metabolize penta and hexahomomethionine. CYP79F1 knockout plants lack short-chain aliphatic glucosinolates, whereas, CYP79F2 knockouts have reduced level of long-chain aliphatic glucosinolates but unchanged level of short chain glucosinolates. CYP79F1 is highly expressed in rosette, stem and silique. CYP79F2 is highly expressed in root.</Comment>
